    "title": "4. Strategy Trial 2: Recovery and Help-Seeking (20%)",
    "name": "Strategy Trial 2: Recovery and Help-Seeking",
    "summary": "Run a second trial focused on recovery, psychological flexibility (adapting when circumstances change), self-compassion, communication, or asking for help. Use a different situation or strategy from Trial 1.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 20% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 20 points.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>PSY355, Fall 2026. Weight: 20 percent.</strong></p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Run a second trial focused on recovery, psychological flexibility (adapting when circumstances change), self-compassion, communication, or asking for help. Use a different situation or strategy from Trial 1.</p><h3>What to submit</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A starting plan linked to a course concept.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Two dated evidence points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One adjustment during the trial.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A reflection on how the situation supported or limited the strategy.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A realistic next action and an appropriate source of support.</li></ul><h3>Boundary</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use a low-risk situation. This is not therapy or a clinical assessment. When a situation exceeds personal coping, name a suitable professional or Seneca support service instead of proposing self-treatment.</p><h3>How your work will be graded</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Strong means 85 to 100 percent of a criterion, Clear means 70 to 84 percent, Developing means 50 to 69 percent, and Limited means 0 to 49 percent.</p><h3>Context and plan: 5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> The trial fits a manageable situation and uses an appropriate concept and support route.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> The plan is clear and appropriate.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> The plan or context is vague.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The plan is unsafe, clinical, or cannot be evaluated.</li></ul><h3>Dated process evidence: 5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> Two useful evidence points and an adjustment are documented.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> Required evidence is present.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> Evidence is partial or weakly connected.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> Process evidence is absent or simulated.</li></ul><h3>Analysis of context: 6 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> The reflection explains how personal, relational, and environmental factors shaped the result.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> The main contextual factors are analysed.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> Context is mentioned but thinly analysed.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The result is individualized or unsupported.</li></ul><h3>Next action: 4 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> The next action is specific, realistic, and includes appropriate help-seeking.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> The next action fits the evidence.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> The next action is vague or weakly supported.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> No usable next action is provided.</li></ul><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Your grade is based on the evidence in your work. Personal disclosure, polished production, and technical effects do not replace course learning.</p><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select <strong>Start attempt</strong>. Write or paste your written work into the <strong>Submission</strong> editor using the headings in these instructions. A Word upload is optional.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Insert your required visual into the editor with a text description, or attach it as an accessible file. If you choose an audio or video format, attach the recording and captions or a transcript. A working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link is also acceptable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Keep all required parts together in this assignment. Before submitting, check that your text, attachments, and links open correctly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select <strong>Submit</strong>, wait for Blackboard’s confirmation, and keep the receipt. If you save a draft instead, return to submit it before the deadline.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Released:</strong> November 9, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due:</strong> November 27, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
