    "html": "<p>Worth 10% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 10 points.</p><h3>Purpose</h3><p>Complete six dated entries across Weeks 2 to 12. Begin each entry with a real learning moment, item from class, or strategy attempt. Then use one idea from psychology covered in this course to explain what happened.</p><h3>Each entry must include</h3><ul><li>A date and specific situation.</li><li>The strategy, decision, or response you tried.</li><li>One course concept, explained accurately.</li><li>Evidence of change, no change, or uncertainty.</li><li>One next action.</li></ul><h3>Format and privacy</h3><p>Aim for 250 to 350 words or a 2 to 3 minute audio entry. The journal is private. You do not need to disclose trauma, diagnosis, health details, or another person’s information. Personal disclosure is never graded.</p><h3>How the journal is graded</h3><ul><li>Dated evidence of your learning process: 2.5 points.</li><li>accurate use of course ideas: 3 points.</li><li>reflection and adjustment: 3 points.</li><li>communication and privacy: 1.5 points.</li></ul><h3>Plan your six entries</h3><p>Complete at least three entries before the Mid-course Reflection so you have dated evidence to use there. Continue with three more entries in the second half of the course. These are planning steps within one graded journal, not extra assignments.</p><h3>How to post your journal entries</h3><ul><li>Open this journal in Blackboard and find <strong>Entries</strong>. Start a separate entry for each required date or example.</li><li>Write directly in the journal editor. Put the entry number and date at the top. You do not need to upload a Word document.</li><li>If you choose an audio entry, attach the recording and a transcript, or add a Seneca-accessible recording link.</li><li>Select <strong>Post</strong>, then reopen the entry to confirm it is there. You may edit your entries before the due date. Only you and the teaching team can read this journal.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p>Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p><strong>Released:</strong> September 14, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p><strong>Due:</strong> December 4, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
